@{
    var faqs = new List<FaqItem>
    {
        new("What is 70 F in Celsius?", "About 21.1 C."),
        new("At what temperature are F and C equal?", "At -40 degrees both scales show the same number."),
        new("Which countries use Fahrenheit?", "Mainly the United States and a few territories.")
    };
}
<h1>@ViewData["H1"]</h1>
<div class="card">
<div class="row"><label>Fahrenheit (F) <input id="a" type="number" step="any" value="98.6" style="width:150px"></label>=<label>Celsius (C) <input id="b" type="number" step="any" value="" style="width:150px"></label></div><p class="muted">Type in either box. The other one updates instantly.</p><div id="out" class="result" aria-live="polite"></div>
</div>

<partial name="_AdSlot" model='"SlotTop"' />

<div class="card">
  <h2>How to use the Fahrenheit to Celsius Converter</h2>
  <p>Type a temperature in Fahrenheit to see Celsius, or type Celsius to see Fahrenheit.</p>
  <h2>About this tool</h2>
  <p>The formulas are C = (F - 32) x 5/9 and F = C x 9/5 + 32. Water freezes at 32 F (0 C) and boils at 212 F (100 C), and normal body temperature is about 98.6 F (37 C). A rough shortcut for Fahrenheit to Celsius is to subtract 30 and halve the result, which is close for everyday temperatures.</p>
  <partial name="_Faq" model="faqs" />
</div>
<partial name="_RelatedTools" />

<script>
pair('a','b',v=>(v-32)*5/9,v=>v*9/5+32);

$('a').dispatchEvent(new Event('input'));
</script>
